course: dictionary
lesson: 3257
title: Equal, level, the same
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you about making things the same. To equalise means to make things equal, or the same, in size, amount or level. «Divers equalise their ears as they go down» means the divers make the push inside their ears even with the push outside. The clue is in the brackets: they pinch their nose and blow gently.
explain: In football, equalise has a related meaning. A goal that makes the score level is called an equaliser, as in «a late equaliser». The other team was ahead, and now the scores are the same again. The clue is the word level, which means equal.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Could you say "make the score level" instead of "equalise"? If it still makes sense, you have found the right meaning. In American English the spelling is equalize, but it is the same word.
fact: Equalise means to make things equal or the same in size, amount or level.
fact: In maths, you can equalise two piles of counters by moving some from the bigger pile to the smaller one.
fact: In UK, Irish and Australian football, an equaliser is a goal that levels the score.
fact: «Divers equalise their ears» means they even out the push inside their ears, for example by pinching the nose and blowing gently.
fact: Divers come up slowly, because shooting up fast can badly hurt them.
exercise: Sara has 9 counters and Tom has 5. She moves 2 counters to Tom's pile so they have the same number. What does this do? | It doubles the piles | It makes the piles smaller | *It equalises the piles | It moves the piles away
exercise: «The striker scored a late equaliser and the crowd went wild.» What does equaliser mean here? | A free kick near the goal | A player who is sent off | *A goal that makes the score level | A goal that wins the game for the other team
exercise: Find and copy the word that means to make things the same. | hill | forest | *equalise | river
test: «Divers equalise their ears as they go down.» Why do divers do this? | To make their ears go red | To stop their hair getting wet | *To even out the push inside their ears | To hear the fish sing
test: Priya says equalise must mean to make a bigger mess. Is she right? | *No, it means to make things equal or the same | No, it means to lose a game | Yes, it means to make things untidy | Yes, it means to make things bigger only
exam: What does an equaliser do in football? | *It makes the score level | It gives a penalty to the other team | It stops the game for a rest | It ends the match early
